        "text": "A hot wallet is any wallet that is connected to the Internet. For example, when you create an account on a centralized crypto exchange and send funds to the wallets you are depositing into the exchange's hot wallets. These wallets are easy to set up, generally requiring no more than downloading software and creating an account. The funds are quickly accessible, making them convenient for new users and frequent traders. Cold wallets, on the other hand, have no connection to the Internet. Instead, they use a physical medium to store the keys offline, making them resistant to online hacking attempts. Cold wallets tend to be much safer for storing your coins and are particularly suitable for buy and hold investors. Another way to categorize wallets is to break them into software and hardware wallets. Most software wallets are hot wallets and can be further broken down into Web wallets, desktop wallets, and mobile wallets, depending on where you access them.",
